/* =====================================================================
   LAYER 7 — THE BELLY OF THE BEAST
   The first true hell layer, below the Shambles. Register: CRONENBERG body
   horror + bathroom horror. Where the Shambles was carnival commerce, the
   belly of the beast is where the demons LIVE, BREED, and REMAKE their bodies — a slum
   of grown flesh and bone, peristaltic, wet, all orifice and transformation.
   The body as architecture, as commodity, as the thing that betrays you. The
   pig-demons (the kidnapper's kind) den and breed here. Holds SIGIL-FRAGMENT 1
   (the Unbinding Sigil; two more lie deeper, in L8 and L9).

   Hub = TheCloaca. Enter from L6 (HellMarket → TheGullet). Descent to L8
   (TheChute) is foreshadowed, not yet open. Depths in setup.depthOf (= 7).
   ===================================================================== */


/* ---------- HUB ---------- */

:: TheCloaca [hell]
<<corrupt "torso" "filth" 2>>
<<nobr>>
The wet stair empties you into the belly through a single great opening, and you
understand the opening for what it is the way you understood the hellmouth: all at
once and too late. A cloaca — the one shared orifice a low animal uses for
everything, waste and birth and the rest — except the size of a railway terminus,
ringed in a muscle that flexes slowly as the deeper dark behind it breathes. This
is the only way in, and the only way out, and the belly uses it for everything.
<</nobr>>

Past the ring the floor falls away into a low wet plaza, and the slum opens around you.
The light is the dull red of a hand held over a lamp, sourceless and even, and by it you
make out the streets — soft-walled, glistening tubes running off into the dark, each one
breathing on its own slow count — and the houses, which are organs, swollen and lit
faintly from within, strung together by ropes of grown gristle. Somewhere a great way off
something the size of a building shifts and settles, and the whole floor moves under you
with it, gently, the way a deck moves, and is still again. You are standing in the gut of
a thing that has made a town of what it eats, and the town is open for the evening.

<<if $corruption gte 70>>
The peristalsis of the place has a rhythm and you find your own breathing has
matched it without asking you, and that the wet warmth on the walls is not
unpleasant, is in fact the temperature of the inside of a mouth, of a body, of
somewhere you have a very old animal memory of having once, before anything, been
safe.
<<else>>
Everything here is grown, not built — the streets are gut, the houses are organs,
the walls breathe and sweat and, here and there, blink. You have come down into a
living thing, and it is digesting a town, and the town does not seem to mind.
<</if>>

<<link "Up the gullet, back toward the Shambles">><<goto "TheGullet">><</link>>
<<link "Along the gutway, the belly's main drag">><<goto "TheGutway">><</link>>
<<link "A still, cold room where nothing grows">><<goto "TheStillborn">><</link>>


/* ---------- ASCENT / DESCENT ---------- */

:: TheGullet [hell]
The gullet is the throat of the wet stair — a ribbed, glistening climb back up
toward the Shambles, and climbing it you go the wrong way up something built only
to swallow, your hands finding purchase on rings of cartilage, the walls closing
warm and close behind you and opening reluctantly ahead.

Halfway up you stop fighting it and let the ribs read you. They are spaced for a
throat's work, each ring a little tighter than the last, and you understand that
nothing has ever climbed this — things go //down// a throat, and the cartilage is
sized for the going-down, and to go up is to make the place do something against its
grain, which it permits the way a sleeper permits a fly. The walls do not strain to
keep you. They simply have not, in their long wet life, been asked to let anything by.

Far up, the light changes from the belly's organ-red to the Shambles' firelit smoke,
and the air loses its intimate, internal warmth and becomes merely infernal, which up
here counts as fresh. Behind and below, the slow swallowing pulse goes on without you,
patient, certain it will have you back down its length the moment you stop climbing.

<<link "Up, into the Shambles market">><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>
<<link "Down, into the cloaca">><<goto "TheCloaca">><</link>>


:: TheChute [hell]
A chute drops out of the belly's floor, and a different hell breathes up it — not
the wet warm organ-heat of the belly but a dry industrial roar, a clang and hiss
and the reciprocating thud of enormous machinery, lit from below by a furnace-red
that pulses on the schedule of some great bellows. The smell that rises is tallow
and scorched hair and the specific reek of a place that processes bodies at scale.
The Rendering Works. The belly grows the meat; down there, something refines it.

You crouch at the lip and look in, and the chute is not stone and not bone but a
gullet of a different make than the wet one behind you — gristled, ringed, the walls
slick with a fat that runs and resets and runs again, polished mirror-smooth by the
long traffic of everything the belly has decided it was done growing. The red light
comes up in waves on the bellows' schedule and you watch it climb the throat toward
you and recede, and each time it climbs you see, briefly, just how far down there is
no purchase at all.

There is no ladder, no stair, no dignified way down — the chute is throat-narrow and
slick and drops sheer into the red, and everything about it tells you that you arrive
in the Rendering Works the way raw material arrives, which is to say //fed in//. But the
second sigil-fragment is down there, in the refining, and you did not come this far to
balk at a slide into a furnace. You can let the chute take you. You will arrive where
the raw material arrives, and you will have to work your way up and out from there.

<<link "Let the chute take you down, into the Rendering Works">><<goto "TheIntake">><</link>>
<<link "Back from the chute, to the sphincter">><<goto "TheSphincter">><</link>>


/* ---------- GATE (guards the sigil-fragment) ---------- */

:: TheSphincter [hell]
The passage to the belly's heart is shut by a sphincter — a ring of dense pale
muscle clenched tight as a fist across the way, weeping a clear slime, flexing
minutely as if dreaming. Through the pinhole at its centre comes a deeper red light
and the sense of something held in the middle of all this flesh that the flesh has
grown around to protect. The fragment is in there. You can feel it the way you feel
a tooth that is about to ache.

<<if $sphincterOpened and not $sphincterWord>>
The sphincter hangs slack and gaping now, worked loose from the muscle side — you came
up through it once from below, from the red Works under the heart, and a one-way muscle
once made to give does not easily learn to clench again. The clean wet road into the
heart stands open, and stays open.

<<link "Through, into the belly's heart">><<goto "TheNavel">><</link>>
<<elseif $sphincterWord>>
You have the privy-shrine's word now — the low wet syllable the belly uses to make
a body open willingly — and you say it, and the sphincter hears it, and shudders, and
//dilates//, slow and total and obscene, opening you a clean wet road into the heart it
was keeping.

<<link "Through, into the belly's heart">><<goto "TheNavel">><</link>>
<<else>>
It will not open for asking, and you do not have the word. You can force it — drive
through the clenched muscle by main strength while it grips and works against you,
and pay for the passage in skin and in something less replaceable — or go and learn
the way to make it open on its own. (A sphincter only fights what comes //in//; force it
this once and it will not close behind you.)

<<set _tlbl to "Force through the clenched muscle (" + setup.tollTag("torso", "filth", 22) + ")">><<link _tlbl>>
  <<corrupt "torso" "filth" 22>>
  <<set $sphincterOpened to true>>
  <<goto "TheNavel">>
<</link>>
<<link "Go and find the word that opens it">><<goto "ThePrivyShrine">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<link "Back to the privy-shrine">><<goto "ThePrivyShrine">><</link>>
<<link "A chute drops out of the floor here, down into a red industrial roar">><<goto "TheChute">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE SIGIL-FRAGMENT ---------- */

:: TheNavel [hell]
The heart of the belly is a navel — a deep, knotted pucker of scar at the centre
of all the grown flesh, where the whole living slum is tied off, where it was joined
once to whatever bore it and then cut loose to grow down here on its own. And set
into the scar, half-swallowed by the proud flesh that has tried for an age to wall
it off and failed, is a thing that does not belong to the body at all: a fragment of
cold incised bone, a third of a mark, a piece of a sigil older than hell and hateful
to it, which is exactly why the flesh has spent so long trying and failing to digest
it.

<<if $sigil.includes("sigil_warrens")>>
The socket where it sat is closing over already, the proud flesh folding in to forget
the wound. You have what you came for. You did not know, until you held it, that you
had come for anything but out.
<<else>>
You know it on sight, the way the body around it knows it: the first piece of the
Unbinding Sigil, the mark that does not free you from hell by climbing but by
*un-belonging* — the thing that, whole, could let a person walk out the front not as
a demon and not as prey but simply as a thing hell has no claim on. One third. The
flesh has kept it like a splinter it couldn't push out. You can.

<<link "Cut the first fragment free of the wall of meat">>
  <<set $sigil.push("sigil_warrens")>>
  <<corrupt "torso" "filth" 4>>
  <<goto "TheNavel">>
<</link>>
<</if>>

<<if $sigil.includes("sigil_warrens")>>
Two more fragments lie deeper — one in the Rendering Works below, one in the Sulphur
Deep at the bottom of everything. Three, and the sigil is whole, and the front door
of hell is just a door.

<</if>>
<<link "Back out through the sphincter">><<goto "TheSphincter">><</link>>


/* ---------- REST ---------- */

:: TheStillborn [hell]
Off the cloaca there is a room the belly failed to finish — a chamber of grown
flesh that died half-made, gone cold and grey and inert, the only dead tissue in a
place that is otherwise horribly, comprehensively alive. Nothing breeds here. Nothing
flexes or weeps or breathes. After the peristaltic warmth of everything else it is a
profound relief: a stillness, a coldness, a place the living slum has given up on and
left alone, the way a body walls off a thing it cannot use.

As your eyes settle you can read what it was trying to be. Everything here is a
beginning that stopped — a ribbed start of wall that meant to go on breathing and
didn't; the grey suggestion of a mouth or a valve that never opened and never will; a
floor part-way to becoming the soft working floor of everywhere else and caught instead
at the texture of cold tallow. The belly started to make this room and then, for some
reason, lost interest, and the half-made thing has held still ever since, waiting to be
either finished or unmade, and getting neither.

You sit against a cold grey wall that was almost an organ and never quite became one,
and the not-breathing of the room lets you remember how to breathe on your own
rhythm again, instead of the belly's. The shaking eases. In the one dead room in the
living dark, you are, briefly, the only thing with a pulse, and for once that is a
comfort.
<<if $hp lt $maxhp>><<run setup.passTime(setup.REST_TIME)>><</if>><<set $hp to $maxhp>>

<<link "A loom of bone, through the cold arch">><<goto "TheBoneLoom">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the cloaca">><<goto "TheCloaca">><</link>>


/* ---------- CACHES ---------- */

:: TheMeatRow [hell]
A market, but where the Shambles sold you tools and gear the meat-row sells //parts//:
stalls hung with the body's inventory, cut and graded and priced — hands by the
bunch like bananas, a rack of ears going cheap, eyes in wet trays sorted by colour,
and the slow patient vendors who are themselves assembled from the overstock,
restocking their own missing pieces from the wares.

You walk the row and the stock walks with you. Not the vendors — the wares. A hand on
its hook curls a finger as your shadow crosses it. The eyes in the trays roll, slow and
wet, to keep you in view as you pass, sorted by colour and all of them following the
same way at once like a field turning to the sun. None of it is dead. That is the
grade the row is sorted to: fresh, which down here means //not yet stopped//, and the
freshest parts are the ones that still flinch from the lamp, and command the best price.

They trade in coin, down here, same as up there. The other currency they prefer not to
mention, because you are, to them, also stock — and you feel the slow patient eyes do
to you what they do to every cut that comes down the row: weigh it, grade it, decide
which shelf, and find no special hurry, because the row is patient and you are already
here.

<<if $meatRowLooted>>
The gutters are picked clean now — you came back for the loose coin, once, when word of
it found you somewhere else. The parts you leave. You have all of yours, for now, and
intend to keep the set.
<<else>>
There's loose coin down in the gutters of the stalls, the kind no vendor stoops for
mid-cut — the kind you only really go after when the thought of it catches you somewhere
else on the floor and walks you back.
<</if>>

<<link "A vendor at the end of the row leans in, in no hurry to cut">><<goto "TheTallyBoy">><</link>>
<<link "A trove of sorted organs, deeper in the row">><<goto "TheOrganTrove">><</link>>
<<link "A surgery, lit and waiting, off the back">><<goto "TheSurgery">><</link>>
<<link "A surgeon's stall at the end of the row, hung with cut-off things">><<goto "TheFleshcutter">><</link>>


:: TheTallyBoy [hell]
A vendor at the very end of the row leans toward you, in no hurry to cut — a slow patient thing built
from the overstock, missing pieces it keeps replacing from its own wares, and running a sideline the
others don't: it trades in what it overhears, across the long economy of taking people apart.

Close up, you can see how it's made. It is more seam than self — a torso off one
donor, an arm off another, a jaw it must have liked the look of and fitted on crooked,
and ears it has too many of, ranked up the side of its head like a thing that wanted to
hear everything that ever got said over a cutting-table. That is the sideline. It stands
here at the quiet end of the row where the talk drifts to, and it keeps every word the
way the other vendors keep the parts, and it will spend a little of the hoard on you if
you let it.

<<if not $meatRowTipped>>
<<linkreplace "Stand by the vendor and let it talk">><<nobr>><<set $meatRowTipped to true>><<corrupt "head" "pig" 1>>
//You'll be going down, sooner or later. They all do.// It weighs you with its eyes, idly, the way
you'd weigh a cut. //Word among the stalls is there's a fire at the very bottom — a tree that burns and
burns and is never the less for it, and the flame off it goes through //anything.// Clog, gate, the fat
that seals the high pipes, the lot. If a body wanted //out// badly enough to go all the way //down//
first.// It leans closer, almost fond. //I do like a face like yours. Already thinking which shelf I'd
put each part of you on. The eyes go quick. Eyes always go quick.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The vendor has gone back to restocking itself from its own wares, and has nothing new for you — only
that idle, sorting look, still deciding which shelf your eyes would go on.
<</if>>

<<link "Back along the meat-row">><<goto "TheMeatRow">><</link>>


:: TheOrganTrove [hell]
Where the row deepens, the trade goes wholesale: a vault of sorted organs in their
hundreds, racked and labelled and kept fresh by the simple expedient of not being
quite dead — livers that flinch from the light, a wall of slow-clenching hearts
ticking out of time with each other, lungs that sigh in a tidal hush when the belly
breathes.

You walk the aisles and the racking is meticulous, which is the worst of it. Each shelf
is labelled in a wet dark script you can almost read, the stock turned so the best face
shows, the soft things bedded on grown shelving that weeps to keep them moist. It is kept
the way a careful grocer keeps a cold store, by someone who takes pride, and the pride is
the horror: that all this was somebody, parcelled out, and that the parcelling was done
with care, and that the care never once extended to asking whether the somebody minded
being so well kept.

Grave-goods of a kind. And in among the meat, the coin that the meat was bought with,
dropped and pocketed and dropped again across the long economy of taking people apart.

<<if $organTroveLooted>>
You've been back for what spends; the coin bedded among the organs is gone, and the wall
of slow-clenching hearts ticks on without it. The organs you leave to their not-dying, and
try not to count how many of each there are, and fail, and wish you hadn't.
<<else>>
The coin the meat was bought with is still bedded in among the racks — dropped and pocketed
and dropped again across the long economy of taking people apart — for anyone who'll reach
in among the slow not-dying to glean it.
<</if>>

<<if not $donorHeard>>
A face among the wall of slow-clenching hearts is watching you — kept fresh by the simple expedient of
not being quite dead — and it is, unmistakably, about to talk; conversational, in no distress at all.
<<linkreplace "A face among the racked hearts is watching you, and seems to want to talk">><<nobr>><<set $donorHeard to true>>
//I'm the face,// it says. //The liver's mine too, three racks over — the shy one, flinches at your
lamp. Lungs by the door. We don't talk much; different shelves.// The hearts tick their out-of-time
time. //The sow-breeder up the way — she'll tell you nothing's wasted, all of that, and true enough.
But it's //louder// the way she tells it, from the outside. From in here it's quieter. It's just being
set //down// — in parts, on different shelves — and not asked to be a whole thing any more. Do you know
how long I carried being a whole thing? How heavy that is, kept up, every hour?// A pause, almost
restful. //Reach in for the coin, if you came for it. We don't grudge it. Grudging was one more piece
they sorted off me, and honestly it was the worst of my weight.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 3>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The face has gone slack among the hearts again, ticking its own slow time; it told you what being kept
feels like from the shelf, and seems content to have you carry it now.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward a well of shed teeth">><<goto "TheToothWell">><</link>>
<<link "Back along the meat-row">><<goto "TheMeatRow">><</link>>


:: TheToothWell [hell]
A well, and the belly has filled it the way a body fills a thing it has no use
for: with teeth. Shed teeth, milk teeth, the long tusks of the pig-things and the
flat grinders of the gorgers and human teeth in their thousands, a deep dry rattling
shaft of every tooth this place has ever pushed out of a gum.

You lean over the lip and the drift goes down past where the light gives out, and it
//moves// — not all at once, but here and there, a settling, a trickle, a single tooth
turning over somewhere far below as the whole mass shifts a hair's breadth deeper, the
way grain shifts in a silo. It is dry in here, the one dry place you have found, and the
dryness has a sound: a constant low rattle and tick, the well sorting itself, the small
hard remainders of every mouth this place has emptied knocking together in the dark. The
warm wet belly above seems suddenly far off, and you a long way down a thing that only
takes.

Drifted and settled and glinting, and salted all through the teeth, the way a child salts
a piggy-bank, is the cold glint of coin.

<<if $toothWellLooted>>
The coin's sifted out of the drift now — you came back and took it, and left the teeth,
which go on being teeth, in the dark, in their thousands, patient as a thing that knows
it will have yours eventually.
<<else>>
Salted all through the teeth, the way a child salts a piggy-bank, is the cold glint of
coin — for whoever's moved, when the thought of it finds them, to plunge an arm into the
rattling dark and sift it out.
<</if>>

<<if not $toothCounterTalked>>
A hunched figure crouches at the well-lip, sifting the dry rattling teeth, sorting them by the gum they
fell from, counting — a creature whose whole remaining self is the count, and who will, if you stop,
look up from it.
<<linkreplace "Stop, and speak to the gleaner at the well-lip">><<nobr>><<set $toothCounterTalked to true>>
//Teeth remember mouths,// it says, not stopping the sort. //And mouths remember //words.// You're
trying to get past the clenched gate, down into the belly's heart — I can see it on you. You don't
//force// it, friend. You can't. You learn to //say// it: the wet syllable that tells the muscle to
open, the one a body kneels for. The privy-idol teaches it — the shrine that's a privy, the privy
that's a shrine. Kneel there, when you're ready to, and it'll put the word in your mouth.// It goes
back to the teeth. //Mind — you'll have to be ready to. The idol can tell when you're still too clean
to mean it.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The gleaner has gone back into the count and will not be drawn out of it again; it told you where the
word is kept, and the teeth rattle on through its fingers, certain of their eventual harvest.
<</if>>

<<link "On, to a weeping wall — a face the size of a house">><<goto "TheWeepingWall">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the organ-trove">><<goto "TheOrganTrove">><</link>>


/* ---------- SNARES (bad ends) ---------- */

:: TheSurgery [hell]
A surgery, off the back of the meat-row, and it is the cleanest room in hell: white
grown-bone tile, a slab, a tray of instruments that are mostly fingers, and a
practitioner folded patiently in the corner that unfolds when you enter — tall,
many-handed, gentle, a Cutter, the kind of demon that doesn't take your face or your
flesh by force but //improves// you, by appointment, with your consent and for your own
good. //You've been making do with the body you were issued,// it says, kindly,
washing its hands in something. //Such a waste. Let me make you fit for down here.//

It can, too. That's the snare of it. Each thing it offers is a real thing — useful,
even elegant — and the cost is only the small human part the new one replaces: the part
that still flinched, that was still squeamish, that was still, technically, //you//. The
belly takes its fee in self, not coin, and what it grows in you is the belly's now,
and stays.

<<set _grafts to setup.graftsAvailable()>>
<<if _grafts.length is 0>>
//There's nothing left of you worth improving,// the Cutter says, with what on a kinder
face would be pride. //You're quite finished. Quite fit.// It folds back into its corner
to wait for the next of you.
<<else>>
It lays its instruments out in a row and names, one by one, what it can give you — and
what each will cost, because down here the cost is the only honest part of the sale:

<<for _gid range _grafts>>
<<capture _gid>>
<<set _git to setup.items[_gid]>>
<<set _glbl to "The " + _git.name + " (" + setup.graftBrief(_gid) + ") — " + setup.tollTag(_git.graftSeg, "pig", _git.graftAmt) + ", and there is no taking it back">>
<<linkreplace _glbl>>
<<set _stage to setup.installGraft(_gid)>>
It works on you with a tenderness that is the worst part of it, narrating each cut in
its kind low voice. When you rise from the slab the //<<= _git.name>>// is yours — <<= _git.desc>> — and it works beautifully. It does not flinch at anything. Neither, by a
little, do you now.
<</linkreplace>>
<</capture>>
<</for>>
<</if>>

<<link "Decline the appointment. Keep the body you came in.">>
  <<soothe 8>>
  <<goto "TheMeatRow">>
<</link>>


:: TheMolt [hell]
The breeding-dens give onto a molting-pit — a warm shallow basin where the belly's
things come to shed, ringed with the discarded husks of what they used to be:
translucent, split up the back, the empty shapes of smaller and more honest creatures
that climbed in here and climbed out as something fitter.

You crouch at the rim and look at the husks the way you'd look at clothes left on a
bank by a swimmer. Each one is a shape you recognize — that one had hands; that one
walked upright; that one, smaller, you do not let yourself finish recognizing — and
each is split clean up the spine and emptied, the seam opened from the inside, willingly,
the way a thing opens that has stopped wanting to be the thing it was. Whatever climbed
out of each husk did not flee. It //graduated//. The husks are not victims. They are
the part that didn't make the cut.

And the pit is //inviting//. It is the temperature of the cloaca, of the inside of a
body, and the deep animal part of you that has been cold and afraid for so long looks at
the warm basin and the split husks and understands, with a lurch, exactly how you would
climb in, and exactly what you would climb out as, and exactly how much better that would
feel.

<<link "Climb into the warm pit. Shed the old self.">><<goto "EndingMolt">><</link>>
<<link "Back away from the molt, skin intact.">>
  <<soothe 6>>
  <<goto "TheBirthingDens">>
<</link>>


/* ---------- COMBAT ---------- */

:: TheSty [hell]
The gutway widens into a sty, and you know the smell before the light finds the shape
of it: wet pork and sewage, the exact smell that closed over your face the night the
sack came down. This is where they breed — the pig-demons, the kidnapper's kind — a
wallow of vast pale sows and their farrow, and the boars that work the belly's
worst, hauling meat to the chute, snatching the solitary off the surface to start
the cycle again. One of the boars has noticed you. It is the size of the thing that
took you. It may //be// the thing that took you.

<<if $styCleared>>
The boar you put down lies in its own wallow, and the rest of the sty has drawn back
from it and from you, the sows curled around their farrow, watching, declining the
fight a single dead boar has taught them to decline. You have killed one of the kind
that kills your kind. It is not nothing. It is not, you suspect, going to be enough.

<<link "Back to the gutway">><<goto "TheGutway">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the breeding-dens">><<goto "TheBirthingDens">><</link>>
<<link "On, to the great latrine">><<goto "TheLatrine">><</link>>
<<else>>
This is a true demon, not vermin, and a wall — it will most likely kill you, and you
should know that going in, and know too that there is a debt here older than your
good sense. You can try it. You can also walk away, the way the surface walks away
from what it can't afford to fight.

<<if not $styRecognized>>
<<linkreplace "Look the boar in the face. Make sure it's the one.">>
<<set $styRecognized to true>><<corrupt "head" "pig" 2>>
You make yourself look, past the bulk and the wet and the small clever eyes, for the
thing you have carried since the sack: a notch bitten from the left ear, an old brand
gone shiny on the shoulder, the particular reek of wet pork that is the last clean
memory you have of the world above. It's all there. It //is// the one — or it is one
of a kind so alike it makes no difference, which is its own horror: that the thing
which ended your life upstairs was not a monster singling you out but a //breeder//
doing rounds, that you were never prey, only stock, a delivery it has half-forgotten
making. It looks back at you with exactly that much interest: the mild, proprietary
once-over a farmer gives an animal that got out, and might be worth the bother of
fetching back.
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

And at its flank something you had taken for a heap of discarded parts unfolds off the
wallow's edge and rises with it — a grafted, the boar's own muck-hand, assembled out of
whatever the dens had spare, too many borrowed arms opening at once. The sty does not
send its breeder out alone.

<<scentof `["pigdemon","grafted"]`>>
<<link "Take the boar — settle the debt (this will likely kill you)">>
  <<run setup.startFight(["pigdemon", "grafted"], "TheStyCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Walk away from the sty, back to the gutway">><<goto "TheGutway">><</link>>
<<link "Skirt the wallow, back to the breeding-dens">><<goto "TheBirthingDens">><</link>>
/* The latrine (and the deep heart beyond it) lies past the boar. Ways by: kill it,
   or get past it. If you're wholly pig it reads you as kin and lets you through clean.
   Otherwise you need the sty-hand's overalls — EARNED by working the dens (TheBirthingDens
   issues "overalls"; the pig-shift corruption is the cost). Having them, the pass is free
   and reusable (you keep the overalls). No overalls and not pig: no sneak, only the dens
   (to work for them) or the fight. */
<<if setup.becameClass() is "pig">>
<<link "Walk straight past the boar, on to the great latrine — it takes you for its own">><<goto "TheLatrine">><</link>>
<<elseif setup.has("overalls")>>
<<link "Pull on the sty-hand's overalls and walk past — one more hand come to muck out, on to the great latrine">>
  <<run setup.equip("overalls")>>
  <<goto "TheLatrine">>
<</link>>
<<else>>
The way past the boar, short of killing it, is to not read as prey — and bare and human-smelling
you are exactly prey, a thing that walked up. The hands pass it daily and it never looks twice,
because they wear the dens' own overalls and move like they've work to do. A set hangs on a hook by
the trough back in the breeding-dens, if you put in a shift to earn it.
<</if>>
<</if>>


:: TheStyCleared [hell]
/* lands here after the pig-demon fight; sets the flag, then folds back */
<<set $styCleared to true>>
The boar goes down the way a building goes down, all at once and from the legs up — its
muck-hand already in parts around it, gone back to being the spares it was assembled
from — and the sty screams — not in grief, you think, but in a kind of recognition, a
passing-up of the news that the solitary thing they farm has, this once, farmed back. You are
shaking. You are also, in a small black chamber of yourself you will not be examining
later, satisfied.

<<link "Back to the gutway">><<goto "TheGutway">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the breeding-dens">><<goto "TheBirthingDens">><</link>>
<<link "On, to the great latrine">><<goto "TheLatrine">><</link>>


:: TheBoneLoom [hell]
A chamber strung floor to vault with a loom of living bone — long spars of it warping
the grown flesh of the belly into new rooms, new walls, new houses, the architecture
of the place weaving itself slowly out of skeleton and meat.

Step in and the loom is working. Not fast — on the scale a glacier moves — but you can
hear it: a slow creak of spar against socket, a wet draw as a fresh length of bone
threads itself through the meat and pulls the weave tighter, the whole frame inching
toward becoming a wall you could not tell from any other wall in the belly. This is how
the place builds. It does not lay one stone on another. It grows the skeleton first and
weaves the rest on after, and somewhere up in the dark of the vault, spars you cannot
see are doing to the ceiling what these are doing to the walls.

And caught in the loom, half-woven into the wall and then somehow //kept//, is a thing
that should have been structure and became a creature instead: the grafted, an assembly
of every spare part the loom had to hand, too many joints and not enough plan, pulling
itself free of the weave it was meant to be and turning the wrongness of its many
borrowed eyes on you.

<<if $boneLoomCleared>>
The grafted lies unstrung, its borrowed parts going slack and separate again, the loom
already beginning to take them back, to try once more to weave a wall and not a thing.
The way deeper hangs open past the loom.

<<link "On, to a weeping wall">><<goto "TheWeepingWall">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the gutway">><<goto "TheGutway">><</link>>
<<else>>
<<scentof "grafted">>
<<link "Cut the grafted out of the loom">>
  <<run setup.startFight("grafted", "TheBoneLoomCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Back to the gutway, away from the loom">><<goto "TheGutway">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheBoneLoomCleared [hell]
/* lands here after the grafted fight; sets the flag, then folds onward */
<<set $boneLoomCleared to true>>
You cut it out of the weave, and it comes apart into its borrowed pieces gratefully,
almost with relief, a hand here and a length of jaw there, each part going still as if
glad to stop being made to be a whole. The loom creaks and resumes, patient, reaching
for the pieces, ready to try again. It is always ready to try again.

<<link "On, to a weeping wall">><<goto "TheWeepingWall">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the gutway">><<goto "TheGutway">><</link>>


/* ---------- PASSAGE / ATMOSPHERE FILLER ---------- */

:: TheGutway [hell]
The gutway is the belly's main drag, and it is exactly what it sounds like: a great
ribbed intestinal tube of a street, peristaltic, the walls slick and rhythmically
working, moving you gently along whether you walk or not the way a gut moves what's in
it.

There is a traffic, and you join it without deciding to. Other things move the
gutway — hunched shapes, hauling shapes, a sow-thing going broad and slow down the
middle — and none of them walk, exactly; they let the wall's slow squeeze pass them
along and only steer, and you find your own feet have learned the trick already, planting
to ride the contraction and not fight it. It is restful. That is the horror of it: how
quickly the body agrees to be moved. The heads in the lining watch you learn it with the
mild incuriosity of the long-digested, fixtures who rode this same current down once and
stopped where they were set.

The floor is soft. You try not to think about where a gut's traffic is, in the end,
always going.

<<if not $lampTalked>>
One of the heads grown into the lining — a streetlamp-head, watching the traffic — tracks you as you
pass, works its jaw, and turns out to have something to say to a thing still walking.
<<linkreplace "One of the streetlamp-heads in the lining tracks you — let it speak">><<nobr>><<set $lampTalked to true>>
//Don't gawp, you'll hold up the line.// It works its jaw, unbothered. //I'm a fixture. Streetlamp. I
watch the traffic past and I don't ask where it's headed — I already know, and so do you, and there's
no overtime in saying it aloud. You'll stop smelling it first; that's how it starts. One shift the
reek's the whole world, the next it's just the air — and that's not the air changed, son. That's you
grown //in.// I never felt the wall come up round me. I was busy. There's always something wants
minding, down a gut.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 2>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The head has gone back to watching the traffic and will not be drawn; it said its piece about the
shift, and a fixture does not repeat itself.
<</if>>

<<link "A sty, where the pig-things breed">><<goto "TheSty">><</link>>
<<link "A loom of living bone">><<goto "TheBoneLoom">><</link>>
<<link "A passage lined with waving villi">><<goto "TheVilli">><</link>>
<<link "A swollen, throbbing chamber — an abscess">><<goto "TheAbscess">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the cloaca">><<goto "TheCloaca">><</link>>


:: TheVilli [hell]
A passage where the walls are villi — millions of soft pale fingers the length of
your arm, waving in a current you can't feel, lining the tube floor to roof, parting
as you pass and closing behind, stroking you along with a gentleness that is its own
horror because it is so clearly //fond//.

The deeper in you go the more they want of you. They are not random in their waving; up
close each one is questing, the tip of it dimpled and damp, and they reach past where
you'd brush them to find the warm parts — the wrist, the throat, the soft inside of the
elbow — and settle there a moment, tasting, before the press of the passage carries you
on and the next million take their turn. None of it hurts. It is closer to being
groomed, or grazed, by something with all the time in the world and a great and patient
appetite, and you walk faster, and faster, and the walls only stroke you the more
warmly for hurrying.

They are absorbing something out of the air, or out of you; you come through the far end
lighter, hungrier, and a little less sure how long the passage was or what it took as
toll for the carrying.

<<link "On, to the breeding-dens">><<goto "TheBirthingDens">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the gutway">><<goto "TheGutway">><</link>>
<<link "A torn gap in the gut-wall, off the passage">><<goto "TheGapCrossing">><</link>>


:: TheBirthingDens [hell]
The dens, where the belly makes more of itself: a honeycomb of warm wet cells,
each holding a labour in progress, the walls translucent and lit from within by the
slow luminous business of things being grown that should not be growing. You do not
look closely. You have learned, down here, the great survival skill of not looking
closely, and you deploy it now with everything you have, and still you come away
knowing more than you wanted about how the meat-row is restocked, and where the
solitary go who are taken off the surface and not seen again.

<<if setup.has("overalls")>>
You have a sty-hand's overalls now — heavy filth-stiff canvas off a dens hook. Worn, with the right
walk, they read to a boar as one more hand come to muck out, not as a thing to fetch.
<<else>>
The farrow go unfed when the sows are deep in labour, and a slop-trough stands at the end of the
row, and the work wants doing whether or not you are the one to do it. The hands who do it wear
heavy canvas overalls against the muck, hung on a hook by the trough — and put in a shift and a set
is as good as yours, which is worth more than it looks: a boar can't tell a thing that wears them
and works from one of its own.

<<linkreplace "Pull a hand's overalls off the hook and work the dens — slop the trough, muck the cells">>
<<set _seg to setup.SEGMENTS.random()>><<set _amt to random(6, 10)>><<corrupt _seg "pig" _amt>>
<<run setup.acquire("overalls")>>
You go down the row with the trough and the muck-rake and do the work — feed the blind grub-pale
farrow that surge and suckle at the slop and at your hands and would take the hands too if the slop
ran out, fork out the spent cells, haul off what the sows are done with. It is a long shift and it
goes into you the way the work always does, the sty settling a degree deeper under the skin. But the
overalls are yours off the hook now, and you have learned the thing they teach: a boar does not
watch a body that's working. It watches a body that's lost. Wear these and move like you've a trough
to get to, and it lets you by.
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<link "The breeder, vast among the cells, turns her head toward you">><<goto "TheBreeder">><</link>>
<<link "A molting-pit, warm and inviting">><<goto "TheMolt">><</link>>
<<link "A sty, where the pig-things breed and wallow">><<goto "TheSty">><</link>>
<<link "Deeper in, to the holding pens, where the kept are fattened">><<goto "TheHolding">><</link>>
<<link "Back along the villi">><<goto "TheVilli">><</link>>


:: TheBreeder [hell]
<<nobr>>
The breeder is the largest living thing in the belly that isn't the belly itself — a
vast pale sow-demon half-grown into the wall of cells she tends, so wide and so settled
that you take her at first for another organ of the place, until the great head comes
round and the small clever eyes find you. She is ringed with farrow and with the
half-finished: things in the translucent cells at every stage of being made, and she
tends them all with the slow, absent tenderness of someone who has done this work
forever and is good at it. She is not angry that you're here. Nothing about you rates
anger. You are simply not yet finished, and she has a great deal of finishing to do.
<</nobr>>

<<if $bredKnown>>
//Back again, half-thing,// she says, not unkindly. //Still loose. Still mostly your own.
You'll keep, a while. They mostly keep, a while.//
<<else>>
//Fresh,// she says, the way you'd say it of fruit, and breathes you in, and seems
satisfied. //Off the surface, and not long off it. Don't fret — everyone comes down a
stranger and goes up nobody. I'll not rush you. Rushing spoils the meat.//
<</if>>

<<if not $bredKnown>>
<<linkreplace "Ask what becomes of the ones taken off the surface and never seen again.">>
<<set $bredKnown to true>><<corrupt "head" "pig" 3>>

//Becomes?// She seems pleased you asked, the way the proud are pleased. //They come here.
All of them, in the end, come here. We take them apart kindly and we put them back
together useful — a boar to fetch the next ones, a sow to grow them, a hundredweight of
good cut for the row, a wall where a wall was wanted. Nothing's wasted. Nothing's even
''lost''; it's only ''rearranged''.// She says it the way the surface says a person has
gone to a better place, and means it just as much.

You understand, with a cold that goes all the way down, that this was meant to be yours:
the sack, the sty, the slow patient table here in the warm — that you were not snatched
by a monster but //harvested// by a process, and that the only thing that has happened,
the only thing, is that you got off the line early.
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
You already know what she does here, and wish, in the front of your skull where her
words have settled and will not leave, that you did not.
<</if>>

<<linkreplace "Ask if she knows the one that took you.">>
She knows them the way a farmer knows which dog works which field. //The boars den down
the gutway, in the sty,// she says, nodding her great head toward it. //If one of mine
fetched you, it'll be there, and it won't recall you any better than the spade recalls
the dirt. Don't take it personal. They don't take ''anything'' personal. That's what
makes them so good at the fetching.//
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "Back away, slow, the way you back from a sow with farrow">><<goto "TheBirthingDens">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE HOLDING PENS (pig-slop, the pig FOOD + the fattening CAPTURE — owner 2026-07-20) ----------
   Where the taken are kept and fattened (the Breeder's "you'll keep, a while"). The trough is the pig
   vice: setup.eatSlop (heal bait / pig cost / feeds the slop addiction). Below the compulsion you eat by
   CHOICE; at pigLimbs>=3 (slopCompelled) the trough draws you and the pen-boars pen you — TheHoldingKept
   (setup.pennedFatten). Leans on the dens' farrow/trough + the Breeder's harvest. */

:: TheHolding [hell]
Past the dens the cells give way to something worse for being so ordinary: a long low run of pens, pen
after pen of the //kept//. These are the taken who did not get off the line — penned close and fed fat,
patient and pink and going quietly to meat, some still with enough face left to look at you as you pass
and some well past that. A trough runs the length of it, and the grey-pink mash in it is the same the
farrow suckle at, and the smell of it reaches right into the back of your throat and //pulls//, because a
part of you — you feel exactly where it sits, and how much of you it is now — has stopped asking what a
thing is before it wants to eat it. This is what the breeder meant. //You'll keep, a while.// This is the
keeping, and here is the larder they keep you on.

<<if setup.slopCompelled()>>
The trough pulls at you the way it pulls at them, and you are already leaning toward it before you have
decided to, and the pen-boars down the run have lifted their heads — not with alarm, there is never any
alarm here, but with the mild proprietary interest of a thing that has just watched a stray wander in
among the stock and begin, visibly, to want its dinner. Cross to it and you will not be a hand who came
to work. You will be a thing that came to feed, and they know precisely what is done with those.
<<link "Drift to the trough — you were never going to do otherwise">><<goto "TheHoldingKept">><</link>>
<<link "Tear yourself away, while a piece of you still wants to">><<goto "TheBirthingDens">><</link>>
<<else>>
There is nothing here to stop you eating. That is the whole horror of the place — not a threat but an
//invitation//: the trough left open, the mash free, the pens patient. You are hungry. You are always
hungry down here, and the surface is a long way up, and it is //right there//, warm and rich and asking
nothing, and no one would blame you, and that is exactly the point of it.

<<linkreplace "Eat from the trough — it is free, and God, you are hungry">><<nobr>>
<<set _r to setup.eatSlop()>>
You get down and you eat, and it is warm and it is rich and it is the best thing you have had since the
sack came down, and it is //good//, and the good has teeth: you feel it settle and take, feel a little
more of you go over to the soft pink patience of the pens even as the ache in your belly lets go.
//(+<<= _r.heal>> vigor, <<= _r.bite>> pig worked into you<<if _r.compelled>>; you are mostly theirs now — the trough will not trouble to ask next time<</if>>)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>

<<link "Back out of the pens, to the birthing dens">><<goto "TheBirthingDens">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheHoldingKept [hell]
<<set _r to setup.pennedFatten()>>
You cross to the trough and you get down among them and you eat, and this time you do not stop, because
the part of you that would have stopped has gone soft along with the rest, and the pen-boars close the run
behind you without hurry and without malice, the way you would shut a gate on a beast that has finally,
sensibly, decided to come in out of the cold. And then it is warm, and it is dark, and there is always
more in the trough, and time goes the way it goes for the kept — thick and pink and undemanding — and you
are fed, and fed, and //fed//.

You do not know how long. You come back to yourself the way you surface from a fever: heavier, slower,
carrying a new weight that is not fat and is not muscle and is, unmistakably, //more of them//. Something
in the run shifts — a lapse in how the boars hold themselves, that mild attention wandering off down the
pens — and in the gap of it you get your legs under you and you //go//, before the keeping closes back
over you for good.
<<nobr>>
//(the pens fatten you: <<= _r.marks.length>> part<<if _r.marks.length neq 1>>s<</if>> of you worked heavy with pig; you come to at half vigor, and get out while you can)//
<</nobr>>

<<link "Claw the rest of the way out, back to the birthing dens">><<goto "TheBirthingDens">><</link>>


:: ThePrivyShrine [hell]
A shrine, and it is a privy — or a privy, and it is a shrine; down here the
distinction the surface insists on has rotted away, and what's left is the truth the
old religions knew and were too polite to keep: that the place where the body voids
itself is a holy place, a threshold, where a person is most alone and most undefended
and most easily met by the things that wait at thresholds.

A pig-snouted idol squats over a hole that goes down forever, slick and venerated, and
the faithful have worn the floor smooth coming here to be unguarded on purpose, in
worship. You come close and the idol is worse than crude — it is //attentive//. The snout
is canted down toward the hole and the worn place before it, where a kneeler would kneel,
and the eyes have been polished to a shine by the press of foreheads, by everyone who has
ever bowed here and put their brow to the cold pig-face to ask it the favour. It does not
look like a thing that is prayed to. It looks like a thing that is //confided in//, that
leans in over the hole the way a confessor leans, to be near the part of you that is too
ashamed to speak above a whisper.

The smell is the privy's own and the floor is the shrine's own, and kneeling here, you
understand, is the price of the question — that the idol teaches nothing to a person
standing up with their guard intact, only to one who has come down to the worn place and
agreed, first, to be seen.

<<if $sphincterWord>>
You have what the shrine teaches. The low wet syllable sits in your mouth like a bad
tooth, and you would spit it out if it weren't the only key you have to the belly's
heart.
<<elseif not setup.inuredEnough()>>
You kneel where the unguarded knelt — and you cannot do the rest of it. What the idol
asks is a loosening, a permission, a deliberate unguarding of the most defended part of
you, and the fresh animal you still mostly are refuses it outright: it clenches, it
recoils, it keeps its one last human dignity by main force. Not yet. The threshold will
still be here when a little more of you has stopped minding.
<<else>>
<<linkreplace "Kneel at the idol. Learn what the threshold teaches.">>
<<set $sphincterWord to true>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 3>>
You kneel where the unguarded knelt, in the smell of the holy privy, and the idol
teaches you the way it teaches everyone: not in words but in a loosening, a permission,
the low wet syllable the body says when it agrees to open. You will be able to make the
sphincter dilate now. You will also, you understand, never quite be able to un-know the
sound your own body makes when it agrees to open, which is the idol's real and patient
point.
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<link "Toward the sphincter, and the belly's heart">><<goto "TheSphincter">><</link>>
<<link "Toward the great latrine">><<goto "TheLatrine">><</link>>


:: TheLatrine [hell]
The great latrine of hell, and it is the bottom of the bathroom horror the whole
descent has been climbing down into: a vast vaulted reeking hall of stalls without
number, every one a threshold, every one occupied or worse waiting to be, where the
damned and the demons both come to be alone with the oldest and most undefended of the
body's businesses, and where the thing that has waited at the privy since before there
were privies — since the first person squatted alone in the dark and felt watched — is
not one lurker in one stall but the whole room, patient, ankle-deep, attending.

You step in and the scale of it undoes you a little. The stalls go back past the lamp's
reach in row on row on row, partitions of grown bone slick to the touch, each doorway a
black mouth and some of them not empty — a shape hunched and busy here, a stillness there
that is worse than busy — and the floor between them is awash, ankle-deep and warm, and
moving, very slightly, against the direction you walk, as if the whole reeking flood were
one slow body turning to keep its face to you. There is no clean tile to stand on. There
is no spot the room is not. To be in here at all is to be in it.

The Šulak's cathedral. You keep your guard up and your business brief and you do not,
under any circumstances, choose a stall.

<<if not $sulakHeard>>
And the whole room speaks. Not one voice from one stall: the room itself, ankle-deep and patient, the
thing that has attended every undefended moment since the first one, addressing you out of all the
stalls at once.
<<linkreplace "Stand still, and let the room speak to you">><<nobr>><<set $sulakHeard to true>>
//I am not in a stall,// it says, from everywhere. //That is the mistake they all make, looking for me
in one. I am the //attending.// I have stood at the threshold of every person who ever crouched alone in
the dark and felt the back of their own neck prickle — that prickle is me; it always was.// The reek
thickens, almost gentle. //You hold your guard so //tight,// fresh thing. It must be exhausting, being
watched and minding it. The ones who last down here are the ones who stop minding — who let themselves
be seen at their most undefended and feel nothing, because there is nothing left in them to defend.
You'll get there. Everyone gets there. I am very patient, and you will be alone in the dark a great many
times before the end.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 3>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The room attends you still, ankle-deep and patient, and says no more — it said the one thing it had to
say, which was that it has all the time there is, and you do not.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward a sty, where the pig-things wallow">><<goto "TheSty">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the privy-shrine">><<goto "ThePrivyShrine">><</link>>


:: TheAbscess [hell]
A chamber the belly has swollen up around some hurt it couldn't resolve — an abscess,
domed and tight and throbbing, the walls stretched thin and shiny and hot to the back
of your hand, the whole room under a pressure you can feel in your fillings, full of a
held and stinking matter the place has walled off rather than dealt with.

The deeper into the dome you go the tighter the air gets, until breathing it is like
breathing through a held breath. The walls here are not the working wet of the gutway;
they are drumhead-taut, stretched to a translucence that lets a sick yellow light through
from whatever is pent behind them, and where you set a hand the membrane is hot and gives,
a half-inch, and pushes back. You can feel the held thing through it — a slow, full,
straining mass with only the wall between you and it, a wall that has been holding, by the
look of the old healed-over splits webbing it, for a very long time and at very great cost.

It is, you realize, the closest thing the belly has to a memory: the one place that hurts,
that the body keeps separate, that it has decided is easier to contain than to heal. You
tread very softly. You would not like to be here when it finally goes.

<<if not $splinterHeard>>
Something in the held matter has noticed you, and is pleased about it — a voice from inside the dome,
delighted to be heard, that you would much rather had stayed walled off.
<<linkreplace "Something in the held matter has noticed you, and is pleased — let it talk">><<nobr>><<set $splinterHeard to true>>
//Welcome to the one room with a //memory,//// it says, from inside the stinking pressure. //Everywhere
else, the filth pools and //agrees// — votes itself into something bigger and meaner; you've felt it
walk you. Not here. //I would not agree.// I stuck in its throat, and it //couldn't// fold me into the
choir, so it built a wall and called it quarantine.// A wet, pleased shifting. //That's a kingdom, that.
My own dome, my own stink, no shift, no sow, no rendering. Beat the whole belly, didn't I.// Then, too
quick, too generous: //Tell you the knack of it, if you like — stay sharp enough and it can't dissolve
//you// either. Lean in, I'll whisper how—// and the dome ticks, under a pressure you feel in your
fillings, and the offer goes nowhere, because a cyst has nothing to teach but how to be forgotten.
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The thing in the dome has talked itself back into its sulk; it offered to teach you nothing, twice
over, and seems to think it won the exchange. The pressure in your fillings does not let up.
<</if>>

<<link "Through to a weeping wall">><<goto "TheWeepingWall">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the gutway">><<goto "TheGutway">><</link>>


:: TheWeepingWall [hell]
A wall that weeps — not water, the way the drains wept, but the body's whole sorry
catalogue of fluids, sweating and seeping and slowly running from a face the size of a
house grown into the belly's flesh, eyes shut, mouth working soundlessly around the
endless wet labour of producing the wet.

You come close, into the run of it, and the face resolves out of the wall it is — a brow
a man could lie down on, a closed eye the size of a door, cheeks runnelled and channelled
by an age of their own weeping, the salt of it crusted white along the gutters it has worn
into the meat below. Underfoot the floor goes from damp to wet to wading as you near it,
the whole catalogue draining down off the great face and pooling and running on to feed
the gutway, the dens, the latrine — every wet room in the belly fed from this one shut-eyed
weeping. It is, you think, where the moisture of the whole belly comes from — one vast grown
gland, crying the slum into being, the way the drowned galleries far above wept themselves
out of one stone face.

The same grief, you think. The same water. Wept and drained and digested and wept again,
all the way down.

<<if not $wallHeard>>
The mouth in the great face is working — not soundlessly now, you realize, leaning close, but around
words, almost: a confession ground out under the endless wet labour, as if the face would tell you the
thing it weeps, if only you put your ear to it.
<<linkreplace "Lean close to the working mouth and listen">><<nobr>><<set $wallHeard to true>>
//Mine,// it weeps. //All of it, mine. The galleries up above — the stone face that wept them — that
was me too, further up, younger. They //made// me to do it: to cry the slum into being, the sweat and
the seep and the wet a place like this runs on. And I cannot stop. I have tried to stop. There is no
stopping a thing you were //grown// to do.// The wet runs faster down its carved cheek. //The same
water, friend. Up there I weep it out, and it drains down, and they drink it back, and it comes to me to
be wept again — all the way down, and round, forever. You're wading in my grief. And soon enough you'll
be a part of how it goes round.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 2>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
You already heard what it had to say, and wish the sound would leave your ear; the mouth works on,
soundless again, weeping the one grief it was grown to weep.
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the loom of bone">><<goto "TheBoneLoom">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the abscess">><<goto "TheAbscess">><</link>>
<<link "A well of shed teeth, through a gap in the meat">><<goto "TheToothWell">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE GAP-CROSSING: a tear in the gut-wall, a spur off the villi passage (owner
   2026-06-08; 2026-06-16 redesign — was a two-way span between the gutway and the dens, now reached
   only from TheVilli). Two ways across the tear, BOTH told on entry: the HIGH bone-bridge (fight the
   wardens once and the span is clear and free forever after, or pay their toll each time until you
   do) and the LOW crevice (filthy, corrupts you each crossing — unless you've a rope to swing the
   clean gap instead). Every way out returns you to the villi ($gapDest/_origin = TheVilli). A
   one-time coin stash sits in a gland at the torn lip. $gapCleared = the wardens are dead for the run. */

:: TheGapCrossing [hell]
<<set $gapDest to "TheVilli">>
<<set _origin to "TheVilli">>
<<nobr>>
The gut-wall has torn clean through here, a ragged gap dropping away into red dark, and the only ways
across are two. High up, a bridge of fused bone arches the span — and from the far end of it comes a
wet, many-jointed shifting, the warden-things the belly keeps to mind its crossings, and they have
heard you come. Low down, where the gap all but pinches shut, a crevice runs wall-to-wall through the
belly's slow seepage, and the reek that breathes up out of it is everything the gut could never
finish: thick and old and patient, and it would be into every seam of you before you were halfway.
<</nobr>>

<<if not $hollowLooted>>
<<linkreplace "(Pick the soft coin from a gland at the torn lip)">>
<<set $hollowLooted to true>><<set _g to setup.gainCoins(random(12, 18))>>
You glean <<= _g>> soft-edged coin<<if _g neq 1>>s<</if>> from the lip — stores the gut swallowed whole and never rendered down.
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

''The high way — the bone-bridge.'' You can hear the creatures up ahead.
<<if $gapCleared>>
The wardens you broke lie scattered the length of the span; the bridge is yours now, and quiet.
<<link "Cross the bone-bridge and back along the villi">><<goto $gapDest>><</link>>
<<else>>
//They'll have a fight, or a toll — ''18 coins'' to be let over.//
<<scentof "grafted">>
<<link "Break the wardens and take the bridge">>
  <<run setup.startFight("grafted", "TheGapCleared")>><<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<if $coins gte 18>>
<<link "Toss them ''18 coins'' and cross unbothered">>
  <<set $coins to $coins - 18>><<goto $gapDest>>
<</link>>
<<else>>(They rattle a cup: 18 coins to cross. You are short.)<</if>>
<</if>>

''The low way — the crevice.'' Filthy, choked with seepage; it would corrupt you to take it.
<<if setup.has("rope")>>
<<link "Swing the clean gap on your rope (the filth stays below you)">><<goto $gapDest>><</link>>
<<else>>
<<set _tlbl to "Squeeze the filth-choked crevice (" + setup.tollTag("torso", "filth", 16) + ")">><<link _tlbl>>
  <<corrupt "torso" "filth" 16>><<goto $gapDest>>
<</link>>
<</if>>

<<link "Step back from the gap">><<goto _origin>><</link>>


:: TheGapCleared [hell]
/* lands here after the bridge-warden fight; sets the clear flag, then crosses to $gapDest */
<<set $gapCleared to true>>
The last of the wardens comes apart into the parts it was assembled from, and they go over the edge
into the red one by one, and the bone-bridge is, all at once, only a bridge. The crossing is yours,
this way and back, for as long as you have need of it.

<<link "Back along the villi">><<goto $gapDest>><</link>>


/* ---------- THE FLESHCUTTER: curse-removal NPC (owner 2026-06-07) ----------
   The one person who can take a cursed item off you. The PRICE is the cut itself: a permanent,
   cross-life SCAR (always a stat-loss, sometimes a worse curse too — setup.cutCurse) plus a modest
   coin fee. You can never leave him clean — only trade a curse you know for one you didn't choose.
   This is the deliberate brake on the "wear cursed power-gear, purify at the end" line. */

:: TheFleshcutter [hell]
<<nobr>>
The stall is hung with the things the surgeon has cut off other people — gauntlets with the hands
still in them, rings on swollen fingers, a coat that twitches when you look away — each one a curse
somebody paid him to be rid of, and each one a reminder that he keeps his work, and that taking a
thing off you is not the same as leaving you whole. Look longer and the wares stop being a trophy
wall and start being a record: here is a man unmade a piece at a time, a relief carved out of other
people's worst bargains, and every freed customer left this stall lighter and walked off short of
something they never got to choose. The surgeon stands at the heart of it, more whole than anything
he sells, and the difference between him and his stock is the toll, taken in scars, that made the
stock. He looks up from his strop with the patience of someone who is never short of customers. //You've something on you that won't come off,// he says,
not a question. //I can help with that. I always leave a little of myself in the work, mind — the
deep likes a cut of every cut. You'll walk out lighter. You won't walk out clean.//
<</nobr>>

<<set _cursed to setup.cursedEquipped()>>
<<if _cursed.length is 0>>
//Nothing on you my knives would know what to do with,// he says, almost disappointed. //Come back
when something's got its hooks in you proper.//
<<else>>
<<for _cid range _cursed>>
<<capture _cid>>
<<set _clbl to "Let him cut off the " + setup.items[_cid].name + " (" + setup.UNCURSE_COST + " coins, and a scar)">>
<<if $coins gte setup.UNCURSE_COST>>
<<linkreplace _clbl>>
<<set $coins to $coins - setup.UNCURSE_COST>>
<<set _scars to setup.cutCurse(_cid)>>
He works fast, and it goes on hurting after he's done. The <<= setup.items[_cid].name>> comes away in
his hand — and what it leaves behind is its own kind of curse, the deep's cut of the cut: //<<= _scars.map(function (s) { return setup.scarLabel(s); }).join("; ")>>//.
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
(He'll take the <<= setup.items[_cid].name>> off you for <<= setup.UNCURSE_COST>> coins and a scar; you are short of coin.)
<</if>>
<</capture>>
<</for>>
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the meat-row">><<goto "TheMeatRow">><</link>>


/* ---------- LAYER-7 BAD END ---------- */

:: EndingMolt [ending]
<<run setup.recordRun("damned")>>
You climb into the warm, and the warm closes over you like the inside of something
that loves you, and you do the thing the husks around the pit all did: you let go of
the seams of the old self, the cold squeamish frightened human shape you have been
hauling through hell like a wet coat, and you let it split up the back, and you climb
out of it.

What climbs out is fitter. It is not cold. It is not afraid. It does not flinch at the
sty or the surgery or the latrine, because those are simply home now, the warm wet
rooms of the body it belongs to. Somewhere behind it in the pit a translucent husk
settles among the other husks, split up the back, the empty shape of a smaller and more
honest creature that came down here looking for a way out, and found, instead, a way to
fit.

//You shed what didn't fit, and what climbed out of the pit belonged. The belly makes
more of itself. Now it has made one more.//

<<link "Again">><<run setup.restartCarry()>><</link>>
